---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import GameCard from '../../components/GameCard.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import games from '../../data/games.json';
import soon from '../../data/coming-soon.json';
import { url } from '../../config';

const language = games.filter(g => g.category === 'language-games');
const jsonLd = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Reading games', item: new URL(url('language-games/'), Astro.site).href },
  ],
}];
---
<Base
  title="Free Reading and Language Games for Kids, Pre-K to Grade 2 | EduLearnLabs"
  description="Free reading games for kids ages 4 to 8: letters, sounds, phonics and first words. Short, kind, ad-free practice that adapts to your child. No login."
  path="language-games/" nav="language" jsonLd={jsonLd}>

  <Breadcrumbs back={{ name: 'Home', path: '' }} items={[{ name: 'Home', path: '' }, { name: 'Reading games', path: 'language-games/' }]} />

  <h1>Free reading and language games for kids</h1>
  <p class="lead prose" style="font-size:20px">Short reading games for Pre-K to Grade 2. Each one practices a single skill,
    like hearing the sounds in a word or spelling it with letter tiles, takes about 2 to 5 minutes and ends with a happy win.</p>

  <div class="grid">
    {language.map(g => <GameCard game={g} />)}
  </div>

  <h2>Coming soon</h2>
  <ul class="soon-list">
    {soon['language-games'].map(g => <li>{g.title} <span class="muted">({g.grade})</span></li>)}
  </ul>

  <AdSlot id="language-mid" />

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>What kids learn, grade by grade</h2>
    <h3>Pre-K (ages 4 to 5)</h3>
    <p>Knowing letters by shape and name, and noticing that words are made of sounds. A child who can point to the
      letter that starts their own name, or say that "sun" and "sock" start the same way, is building the base for
      reading. At this age it is about play and noticing, not about getting every letter right.</p>
    <h3>Kindergarten (ages 5 to 6)</h3>
    <p>Matching each letter to its sound, hearing the first, middle and last sound in a short word, and reading a few
      sight words like "the", "and" and "is" at a glance. Kids learn that letters stand for sounds, and that sounds put
      together make words.</p>
    <h3>Grade 1 (ages 6 to 7)</h3>
    <p>Blending sounds into words and spelling short words by their sounds: c, a, t makes cat. Then words with two
      consonants together, like frog and stop, and longer picture words like truck. This is where
      <a href={url('language-games/word-building-for-grade-1/')}>Word Building for Grade 1</a> fits: children hear a word,
      look at its picture and drag letter tiles into place from left to right, then hear the word they built.</p>
    <h3>Grade 2 (ages 7 to 8)</h3>
    <p>Spelling longer words of 4 to 6 letters, finishing sentences with the right word, and sorting words into
      naming words and doing words (nouns and verbs). Reading moves from sounding out to reading for meaning.</p>

    <h2>Learning goals</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sounds first.</strong> Hear the separate sounds in a spoken word, the skill reading experts call
        phonemic awareness.</li>
      <li><strong>Sound to letter.</strong> Know which letter, or pair of letters, writes each sound.</li>
      <li><strong>Left to right.</strong> Build and read words in order, first sound first.</li>
      <li><strong>Word meaning.</strong> Connect a written word to a picture and to the thing it names.</li>
      <li><strong>Confidence.</strong> Try a word, get a gentle hint, try again, and finish with a word that is right.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How our reading games work</h2>
    <p><strong>One skill, one game.</strong> A spelling game only asks your child to spell. Clear practice helps a
      child feel progress, and it helps you see what they can do.</p>
    <p><strong>Hear it, see it, build it.</strong> Every word is spoken out loud and shown as a picture, so early
      readers can play alone. Children move real letter tiles instead of picking from a list, so the game shows how
      sounds turn into letters.</p>
    <p><strong>Hints, never the answer.</strong> A letter in the wrong place simply hops back. Hazel the owl can say
      the word again, show where the next letter goes, or light up the letters that belong. The child always places
      the last letter themselves, and nobody sees a failure screen.</p>
    <p><strong>Just right difficulty.</strong> A few words right in a row and the game steps up, from three letter
      words to longer ones. A couple of tricky words and it steps back. Words your child found hard come back next time.</p>
    <p><strong>Mistakes that teach us something.</strong> Mixing up b and d, or the middle sound in cat and cut, is
      normal at this age. The <a href={url('grown-ups/')}>Grown-ups page</a> turns a slip that keeps coming back
      into a simple tip you can try together.</p>

    <h2>How parents can help</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Play one game together first. Say the word slowly, stretch the sounds, "sssun", and let your child find the letters.</li>
      <li>Say letter sounds, not letter names, when you help: "c" as in cat, not "see".</li>
      <li>Short and often beats long and rare. One game a day is plenty.</li>
      <li>Praise effort: "You listened for every sound" helps more than "You are so smart".</li>
      <li>Read together every day, even for five minutes. Games practice a skill; stories give a reason to read.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Want some numbers too? Try <a href={url('math-games/addition-for-grade-1/')}>Addition for Grade 1</a> and
      <a href={url('math-games/subtraction-for-grade-1/')}>Subtraction for Grade 1</a>, or see all
      <a href={url('math-games/')}>free math games</a>.</p>

    <h2>Questions parents ask</h2>
    <h3>Are the reading games really free?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Every game is free to play, with no ads inside games, no in-game purchases and nothing to unlock with money.</p>
    <h3>My child cannot read yet. Can they still play?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Every word is spoken aloud and has a picture, and the buttons use pictures and short words. The games are
      made for children who are just starting to read.</p>
    <h3>Does my child need an account?</h3>
    <p>No. There is no login and no sign up. Progress is saved only in this browser on this device, and you can
      clear it any time on the Grown-ups page.</p>
    <h3>Which devices work?</h3>
    <p>Any modern browser: tablets, phones held sideways, laptops and Chromebooks. The games are small and load in a few
      seconds, even on older school computers and slow connections.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
